+\subsubsection{Learned Methods}
+
+\paragraph{Attention Model Policy (Neural Agent).}
+The framework provides an Attention Model adapter~\cite{Kool2019AttentionModel}
+for learned constructive routing, exposing neural policies through the same
+single-period constructor interface as the classical algorithms. It operates on
+normalized sensor fill levels scaled to the unit interval and decodes visits
+sequentially. The implementation adapts the baseline architecture of Kool et
+al.~\cite{Kool2019AttentionModel}: decoder queries omit static global graph
+pooling, driving the step context by the current node embedding and unvisited
+waste, while pointing logits are computed via multi-head dot products scaled by
+the square root of the head dimension and averaged across heads before applying
+tanh clipping with a clipping magnitude of 10. Dynamic action masking enforces
+mandatory selection by excluding the depot until every node in the mandatory
+set $\mathcal{M}^t$ has been visited, and masks visited as well as
+nonpositive-waste optional bins; it does not enforce vehicle capacity. Decoding
+proceeds either greedily or by temperature-controlled sampling.
+
+The learned model is excluded from the reported benchmark because of poor results
+on orienteering-type problems, including the VRPP, where routing and subset
+selection interact. Changes to its training regimen and architecture are
+pending. The reported municipal benchmark therefore evaluates only the eight
+classical constructors.
+
